WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.717 --> 00:00:04.884
- You have to, I believe,
let small, bad things happen

00:00:05.927 --> 00:00:09.180
constantly to have any agenda of your own,

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.976
and to get the big, positive things done.

00:00:10.976 --> 00:00:12.447
And if you're like, I don't have time,

00:00:12.447 --> 00:00:14.288
I can't sleep, I need
to cut back on sleep,

00:00:14.288 --> 00:00:15.956
I need to do this, I need to do that,

00:00:15.956 --> 00:00:19.048
if you don't have time,
you don't have priorities.

00:00:19.048 --> 00:00:19.881
That's it.

00:00:19.881 --> 00:00:22.756
Perfection is achieved not when
there's nothing more to add,

00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:24.915
but when there's nothing
more to take away.

00:00:24.915 --> 00:00:28.005
- He's an American author,
entrepreneur, and public speaker.

00:00:28.005 --> 00:00:29.962
He's written a number of self-help books

00:00:29.962 --> 00:00:31.632
on the Four-Hour theme.

00:00:31.632 --> 00:00:33.981
He's also a successful angel investor.

00:00:33.981 --> 00:00:36.017
He's Tim Ferriss, and
here's my take on his

00:00:36.017 --> 00:00:38.500
Top Ten Rules for Success, Volume 2.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.491
Rule number six is my personal favorite,

00:00:40.491 --> 00:00:42.685
and I'd love to know which
one you guys like the best.

00:00:42.685 --> 00:00:44.105
And as always, as you're watching,

00:00:44.105 --> 00:00:46.421
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:46.421 --> 00:00:48.097
please leave it in the comments below.

00:00:48.097 --> 00:00:49.005
Put quotes around it

00:00:49.005 --> 00:00:50.852
so other people can be inspired, as well,

00:00:50.852 --> 00:00:52.190
and when you write it down,

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.873
it's much more likely to
stick with you, as well.

00:00:54.873 --> 00:00:55.706
Enjoy.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.503
(upbeat theme music)

00:01:09.015 --> 00:01:11.822
- I find that levels of success

00:01:11.822 --> 00:01:14.397
in almost any industry or area

00:01:14.397 --> 00:01:18.052
correlate to a person
asking great questions.

00:01:18.052 --> 00:01:19.662
In some cases, they seem absurd.

00:01:19.662 --> 00:01:22.029
In fact, the hallmark, in
a way, of great questions

00:01:22.029 --> 00:01:24.509
is they sound completely ridiculous.

00:01:24.509 --> 00:01:26.622
So someone might ask, like
Peter Thiel, why can't you

00:01:26.622 --> 00:01:30.489
achieve your 10-year goals
in the next six months?

00:01:30.489 --> 00:01:31.476
As a thought exercise,

00:01:31.476 --> 00:01:34.847
that's a good question
to answer for yourself.

00:01:34.847 --> 00:01:39.509
And these types of questions
come up surprisingly often

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:43.417
with very, very impressive
folks, whether it's in business,

00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:45.721
military, entertainment, or otherwise.

00:01:45.721 --> 00:01:49.219
And the way you can get
better at questions is by

00:01:49.219 --> 00:01:51.543
studying interviews, in part.

00:01:51.543 --> 00:01:54.804
So I studied Larry King.
I studied Charlie Rose.

00:01:54.804 --> 00:01:56.349
I studied Terry Gross.

00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:59.480
I studied da, da, da,
da, go down the list.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:02.732
I studied Tony Robbins,
who does in-person,

00:02:02.732 --> 00:02:05.299
one-on-one interventions at his events

00:02:05.299 --> 00:02:08.104
in front of 10,000, 5,000 people,

00:02:08.104 --> 00:02:11.193
and he's just genius with
how he uses questions

00:02:11.193 --> 00:02:14.411
to pattern-interrupt and
grab someone's attention,

00:02:14.411 --> 00:02:17.786
and divert them in a more
productive direction.

00:02:17.786 --> 00:02:18.761
Now, why is that relevant?

00:02:18.761 --> 00:02:20.795
It's relevant because

00:02:20.795 --> 00:02:23.809
thinking is the process
of asking questions.

00:02:23.809 --> 00:02:25.112
You're asking yourself questions,

00:02:25.112 --> 00:02:26.698
and then you're answering
them in your own head.

00:02:26.698 --> 00:02:29.179
So if you get better at
asking other people questions,

00:02:29.179 --> 00:02:31.220
you get better at asking
yourself questions,

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:35.387
which means you are improving
your thought performance

00:02:36.632 --> 00:02:38.789
and level of thinking.

00:02:38.789 --> 00:02:41.802
When I'm procrastinating
because there's indecision,

00:02:41.802 --> 00:02:44.984
and this is a particular
breed of procrastination,

00:02:44.984 --> 00:02:48.076
in other words, if I have
10 things on my to-do list,

00:02:48.076 --> 00:02:51.087
or 10 potential projects I could pursue,

00:02:51.087 --> 00:02:52.791
what to do in that situation?

00:02:52.791 --> 00:02:57.140
And what I ask myself is,
which one of these, if done,

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:00.555
will make the rest irrelevant or easier?

00:03:00.555 --> 00:03:02.712
This is a key question I ask all the time.

00:03:02.712 --> 00:03:05.069
Which one of these will make
all the rest easier to do,

00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:07.189
if done first, or, all of
the rest of them irrelevant,

00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:08.703
don't even need to do them.

00:03:08.703 --> 00:03:12.686
That is how I will then hone
in on one piece of the puzzle.

00:03:12.686 --> 00:03:14.966
And this can be applied
all over the place.

00:03:14.966 --> 00:03:17.129
But let's just say it's
the developing a podcast.

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:19.251
It could be losing weight, all right?

00:03:19.251 --> 00:03:20.999
You can see that's very, very amorphous,

00:03:20.999 --> 00:03:24.541
when you need timelines, when
you need an amount to lose.

00:03:24.541 --> 00:03:25.844
And then you want to make
it as small as possible.

00:03:25.844 --> 00:03:27.635
So I'll give you a different example.

00:03:27.635 --> 00:03:29.511
If you want to start flossing your teeth,

00:03:29.511 --> 00:03:30.731
who likes flossing their teeth?

00:03:30.731 --> 00:03:32.031
Pretty much nobody.

00:03:32.031 --> 00:03:33.698
So how do you start flossing your teeth?

00:03:33.698 --> 00:03:36.101
Well, you want to make
it as easy as possible

00:03:36.101 --> 00:03:39.274
to develop as part of
your routine, to make it

00:03:39.274 --> 00:03:43.340
as automatic as anything else
that you do consistently.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.249
You can borrow from, say, BJ Fogg,

00:03:45.249 --> 00:03:47.897
who's done a lot of research
at Stanford and elsewhere,

00:03:47.897 --> 00:03:51.275
make it as small as possible,
meaning, in the beginning,

00:03:51.275 --> 00:03:53.517
do less than you are capable of doing.

00:03:53.517 --> 00:03:54.775
This is another key.

00:03:54.775 --> 00:03:57.665
When you think something
is too big or onerous,

00:03:57.665 --> 00:03:58.888
so it's too intimidating,

00:03:58.888 --> 00:04:01.048
or it's too much of a pain in the ass.

00:04:01.048 --> 00:04:02.313
So for flossing, you might say,

00:04:02.313 --> 00:04:05.117
I'm only going to floss
my front two teeth.

00:04:05.117 --> 00:04:08.043
That's three gaps. That's
all you're going to do.

00:04:08.043 --> 00:04:11.176
And you want to make it,
again, as easy as possible.

00:04:11.176 --> 00:04:13.331
So you might use a Water Pik.

00:04:13.331 --> 00:04:16.299
Or you might use those
disposable flossing gadgets

00:04:16.299 --> 00:04:19.024
so you don't have to do

00:04:19.024 --> 00:04:21.161
tourniquets on your fingers,

00:04:21.161 --> 00:04:23.969
which is also one of the
side effects of flossing

00:04:23.969 --> 00:04:25.271
that deters people.

00:04:25.271 --> 00:04:26.939
Make it as easy as possible.

00:04:26.939 --> 00:04:29.254
Now this applies to a
lot more than flossing.

00:04:29.254 --> 00:04:31.942
I've talked to many of the people

00:04:31.942 --> 00:04:33.185
for, say, Tools of Titans,

00:04:33.185 --> 00:04:35.988
people who are eight-time New York Times

00:04:35.988 --> 00:04:37.616
best-selling authors,

00:04:37.616 --> 00:04:40.257
or prolific musicians,

00:04:40.257 --> 00:04:42.576
prolific music producers like Rick Rubin,

00:04:42.576 --> 00:04:44.363
who's legendary.

00:04:44.363 --> 00:04:47.906
And it all comes down to
tiny homework assignments.

00:04:47.906 --> 00:04:49.779
So Rick, if he has a stuck
artist, for instance,

00:04:49.779 --> 00:04:54.251
he will say, can you get
me one word or one line

00:04:54.251 --> 00:04:57.550
that you might like for this
song that you're working on,

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:58.488
by tomorrow?

00:04:58.488 --> 00:04:59.832
Is that possible?

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:01.584
Mini, mini homework
assignments, all right?

00:05:01.584 --> 00:05:04.111
So with a creative project,
in the beginning, that's one.

00:05:04.111 --> 00:05:05.531
It's related to a piece
of advice that I got

00:05:05.531 --> 00:05:07.980
from Neil Strauss,
eight-time New York Times

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:09.327
best-selling author.

00:05:09.327 --> 00:05:10.794
He has written for the New York Times.

00:05:10.794 --> 00:05:13.719
He's written for Rolling Stone magazine.

00:05:13.719 --> 00:05:16.652
And that is, lower your standards.

00:05:16.652 --> 00:05:18.573
He doesn't believe in writer's block.

00:05:18.573 --> 00:05:19.913
He says, your standards are just too high.

00:05:19.913 --> 00:05:22.800
You're creating performance
anxiety for yourself.

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:25.935
So the advice that I
got from another writer,

00:05:25.935 --> 00:05:30.086
which matches with that, is
two crappy pages per day.

00:05:30.086 --> 00:05:32.002
So a lot of people are
like, I'm going to kill it.

00:05:32.002 --> 00:05:33.142
I need an ambitious goal.

00:05:33.142 --> 00:05:36.360
Let me do 1,500 words, 2,000 words per day

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:38.563
for this book I'm working on.

00:05:38.563 --> 00:05:41.489
Well, there is a very high probability

00:05:41.489 --> 00:05:43.278
that you're going to fall short of that,

00:05:43.278 --> 00:05:45.472
and then you will get demoralized,

00:05:45.472 --> 00:05:47.261
then you will get intimidated by the task,

00:05:47.261 --> 00:05:49.173
and then you will start procrastinating.

00:05:49.173 --> 00:05:53.605
So make the hurdle, make
the success threshold

00:05:53.605 --> 00:05:54.585
really, really low.

00:05:54.585 --> 00:05:57.395
That's what I've done
for my last three books,

00:05:57.395 --> 00:05:58.982
is two crappy pages per day.

00:05:58.982 --> 00:05:59.815
That's all I need.

00:05:59.815 --> 00:06:01.101
If I don't end up using them, that's fine.

00:06:01.101 --> 00:06:03.216
I just need to get out two crappy pages.

00:06:03.216 --> 00:06:05.572
What ends up happening, with the flossing,

00:06:05.572 --> 00:06:08.642
with the writing, with, say, exercise?

00:06:08.642 --> 00:06:10.467
If you're going to exercise,
you're making a New Year's

00:06:10.467 --> 00:06:12.908
resolution, don't make it an
hour a day, four times a week.

00:06:12.908 --> 00:06:14.007
No, no, no, no, no.

00:06:14.007 --> 00:06:16.284
And if you don't have an exercise habit,

00:06:16.284 --> 00:06:20.961
five to 10 minutes at the gym,
three times a week, plenty.

00:06:20.961 --> 00:06:23.966
And in all those cases,
you will feel successful,

00:06:23.966 --> 00:06:27.546
because you've checked
your box for success,

00:06:27.546 --> 00:06:28.778
and then, very often,

00:06:28.778 --> 00:06:30.852
you will exceed that
for extra credit, right?

00:06:30.852 --> 00:06:31.828
Well, I'm already at the gym,

00:06:31.828 --> 00:06:32.661
I'll go for an extra 10 minutes.

00:06:32.661 --> 00:06:33.497
Well, I'm already flossing my teeth,

00:06:33.497 --> 00:06:34.963
I'll do an extra four.

00:06:34.963 --> 00:06:36.922
Well, I've already hit my two pages,

00:06:36.922 --> 00:06:38.634
but I'm feeling great and I'm in the flow.

00:06:38.634 --> 00:06:40.869
Maybe I'll do 10, maybe I'll do 20.

00:06:40.869 --> 00:06:44.162
But it prevents you from
feeling like a failure.

00:06:44.162 --> 00:06:45.181
This is very, very important.

00:06:45.181 --> 00:06:47.217
That is what derails a lot of people.

00:06:47.217 --> 00:06:49.905
And it also makes the
task less intimidating.

00:06:49.905 --> 00:06:53.569
It's easy to discard,
say, 10 terrible ideas

00:06:53.569 --> 00:06:55.488
and go after the one good idea,

00:06:55.488 --> 00:06:58.613
but when you start to get
a little bit of momentum,

00:06:58.613 --> 00:07:01.950
you can drown yourself
in good opportunities

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:03.458
that aren't great opportunities.

00:07:03.458 --> 00:07:04.891
And if you scatter your focus,

00:07:04.891 --> 00:07:08.430
you try to do 17 different product lines,

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:10.507
you can kill your business really easily,

00:07:10.507 --> 00:07:12.501
particularly when you have a small team.

00:07:12.501 --> 00:07:14.987
So I think asking yourself, repeatedly,

00:07:14.987 --> 00:07:17.272
what is the one project,
the one initiative,

00:07:17.272 --> 00:07:19.879
the one campaign that, if successful,

00:07:19.879 --> 00:07:23.831
will render the rest of these
things either unnecessary

00:07:23.831 --> 00:07:25.216
or much, much easier?

00:07:25.216 --> 00:07:27.049
What is that one step?

00:07:28.272 --> 00:07:30.024
And I've called it this
lead domino before,

00:07:30.024 --> 00:07:31.980
but what is the one thing on this list

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:33.768
of seven different
campaigns that will make

00:07:33.768 --> 00:07:36.696
all the other ones
irrelevant or much easier?

00:07:36.696 --> 00:07:39.215
- That's a tough answer, isn't it?

00:07:39.215 --> 00:07:41.457
Because they all sound good.

00:07:41.457 --> 00:07:43.817
- Yeah, I think they can.

00:07:43.817 --> 00:07:47.684
And I think that what it
comes down to, oftentimes is,

00:07:47.684 --> 00:07:48.982
it returns back to measurement.

00:07:48.982 --> 00:07:51.182
So how are we defining success?

00:07:51.182 --> 00:07:53.908
If we want to grow the
company, let's just say,

00:07:53.908 --> 00:07:54.741
what does that mean?

00:07:54.741 --> 00:07:57.605
In three months, six months,
what are we measuring?

00:07:57.605 --> 00:08:00.328
Why are we measuring those things?

00:08:00.328 --> 00:08:04.308
And what is a sort of
comfort goal, meaning,

00:08:04.308 --> 00:08:06.704
okay, we think we can
easily hit this number.

00:08:06.704 --> 00:08:09.511
What is a stretch and what is, like,

00:08:09.511 --> 00:08:12.279
hallelujah, we threaded the needle?

00:08:12.279 --> 00:08:16.872
And then come up with a really
concrete number to tackle.

00:08:16.872 --> 00:08:17.810
Once you have that number,

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:19.274
then you can look at those
five and say, all right,

00:08:19.274 --> 00:08:21.308
which of those are going
to serve us right now?

00:08:21.308 --> 00:08:24.523
And I'm dealing with
that, increasingly so,

00:08:24.523 --> 00:08:27.712
because I have all
these different branches

00:08:27.712 --> 00:08:30.722
of content and activities and
angel investing and so on.

00:08:30.722 --> 00:08:32.200
It's very easy for me to get scattered.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:33.334
It's never been easier.

00:08:33.334 --> 00:08:35.122
So I have to continually
ask that type of question.

00:08:35.122 --> 00:08:36.713
- What's your main focus now?

00:08:36.713 --> 00:08:39.316
If you had to boil it down to one?

00:08:39.316 --> 00:08:41.649
- My main focus right now is

00:08:42.695 --> 00:08:45.868
building my podcast, the Tim Ferriss Show,

00:08:45.868 --> 00:08:47.454
up to a point where it's consistently

00:08:47.454 --> 00:08:49.895
in the top 10 to 15 on iTunes,

00:08:49.895 --> 00:08:53.026
so that I can establish a
presence and name recognition

00:08:53.026 --> 00:08:55.224
in Hollywood in entertainment,
which I can leverage, then,

00:08:55.224 --> 00:08:58.686
for the TV show and film
projects that I'll be

00:08:58.686 --> 00:09:01.293
expanding into in the
next three to nine months.

00:09:01.293 --> 00:09:05.727
And specifically what that
means is, targeted advertising

00:09:05.727 --> 00:09:09.147
for the podcast,
specifically towards people

00:09:09.147 --> 00:09:12.363
who are producers,
agents, actors, directors

00:09:12.363 --> 00:09:14.031
in Hollywood and New York City.

00:09:14.031 --> 00:09:15.657
All of that can be quantified.

00:09:15.657 --> 00:09:18.465
- [Woman] Timothy, how do I
get a four-hour work week?

00:09:18.465 --> 00:09:19.767
Is that possible?

00:09:19.767 --> 00:09:21.970
- The four-hour work week is possible,

00:09:21.970 --> 00:09:25.637
but you need to completely
unplug and reset.

00:09:26.897 --> 00:09:29.535
And the reason that's
necessary is because there is

00:09:29.535 --> 00:09:32.831
an epidemic, and I do mean
epidemic in this country,

00:09:32.831 --> 00:09:36.330
of information abuse and
information addiction,

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:38.684
where people have come to believe that

00:09:38.684 --> 00:09:40.797
checking email 200 times per day,

00:09:40.797 --> 00:09:43.119
having a Blackberry to
your head or in your hand

00:09:43.119 --> 00:09:44.665
while you're at dinner or on the subway

00:09:44.665 --> 00:09:46.824
or in your car or with your friends,

00:09:46.824 --> 00:09:49.347
is the path to becoming more
productive and more successful.

00:09:49.347 --> 00:09:50.850
- [Woman] You mean, it isn't?

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:54.148
- It isn't, because
giving everyone around you

00:09:54.148 --> 00:09:57.488
every person in the world
immediate access to you

00:09:57.488 --> 00:09:59.973
is inviting interruption
and inviting minutia

00:09:59.973 --> 00:10:02.011
to completely invade your life,

00:10:02.011 --> 00:10:03.592
which is happening to everyone.

00:10:03.592 --> 00:10:04.894
- [Woman] Did it happen to you?

00:10:04.894 --> 00:10:05.991
- It did happen to me.

00:10:05.991 --> 00:10:07.618
I had no intention of writing this book,

00:10:07.618 --> 00:10:10.101
but from 2000 to 2004,

00:10:10.101 --> 00:10:11.937
I was working at startups
in Silicon Valley.

00:10:11.937 --> 00:10:14.140
I started my own, I was the CEO,

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:16.699
and I worked from 7:00 AM to 9:00 PM.

00:10:16.699 --> 00:10:20.119
Every day, I checked
Outlook, hitting Send/Receive

00:10:20.119 --> 00:10:21.623
100 to 200 times per day,

00:10:21.623 --> 00:10:24.599
like a rat with a
cocaine pellet dispenser.

00:10:24.599 --> 00:10:25.736
Slept under my cubicle.

00:10:25.736 --> 00:10:28.548
Sent emails on Thanksgiving to prospects.

00:10:28.548 --> 00:10:30.500
It was a depressing scene.

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:33.787
And it's a very,
unfortunately, common scene.

00:10:33.787 --> 00:10:36.314
And I think everyone is
at a point of overwhelm.

00:10:36.314 --> 00:10:39.002
There is more information
than we can possibly organize.

00:10:39.002 --> 00:10:41.030
Time management is dead.

00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:43.718
- [Woman] Well, how do
you turn that around?

00:10:43.718 --> 00:10:45.223
- The way to turn it around is,

00:10:45.223 --> 00:10:48.600
you have to completely unplug and reset.

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:50.960
That means you need to take a step back.

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:52.833
Forget about what people expect you to do.

00:10:52.833 --> 00:10:55.154
Forget about what's
popular, and really look

00:10:55.154 --> 00:10:58.735
at what works and what
is consuming your time.

00:10:58.735 --> 00:11:00.201
There are four steps.

00:11:00.201 --> 00:11:04.882
There's definition, elimination,
automation, and liberation.

00:11:04.882 --> 00:11:05.978
Definition is simple.

00:11:05.978 --> 00:11:08.950
First, you need to define
your ideal lifestyle.

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:11.111
What do you want to be
doing from when you wake up

00:11:11.111 --> 00:11:13.835
to when you go to sleep?

00:11:13.835 --> 00:11:15.623
What do you want to have?

00:11:15.623 --> 00:11:18.352
What do you want to be?
What do you want to do?

00:11:18.352 --> 00:11:20.591
And how much does that
ideal lifestyle cost?

00:11:20.591 --> 00:11:23.030
And that becomes your target.

00:11:23.030 --> 00:11:24.333
Elimination is simple.

00:11:24.333 --> 00:11:26.449
It's getting rid of
everything, all the static,

00:11:26.449 --> 00:11:28.402
all the noise, all the interruptions,

00:11:28.402 --> 00:11:31.088
all the micromanaging,
all the people possible

00:11:31.088 --> 00:11:34.214
that interfere with getting
into that ideal lifestyle.

00:11:34.214 --> 00:11:36.534
The third, automation,
is about taking the few

00:11:36.534 --> 00:11:40.158
remaining tasks that are
important but time-consuming,

00:11:40.158 --> 00:11:42.893
and either delegating, automating,

00:11:42.893 --> 00:11:45.250
or somehow outsourcing them.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:49.195
In my particular case, I have
an army of MBAs in India,

00:11:49.195 --> 00:11:51.595
about 25 of them who
work for $4.00 an hour,

00:11:51.595 --> 00:11:53.710
and take care of tasks
that otherwise consume

00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:55.870
hundreds of my hours.

00:11:55.870 --> 00:11:57.779
And then the last step, liberation,

00:11:57.779 --> 00:12:00.383
is about the final ingredient
in lifestyle design,

00:12:00.383 --> 00:12:02.090
which is mobility, and then also,

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:03.839
how to use the time once you create it,

00:12:03.839 --> 00:12:05.464
which is very difficult for most people.

00:12:05.464 --> 00:12:07.338
- [Woman] Okay, this is
fascinating, but that's the point.

00:12:07.338 --> 00:12:08.394
It is difficult.

00:12:08.394 --> 00:12:10.188
How do you put the Blackberry down?

00:12:10.188 --> 00:12:11.817
Aren't you worried that
you're not going to make

00:12:11.817 --> 00:12:14.503
as much money, that you're
going to lose clients?

00:12:14.503 --> 00:12:16.253
Do you have statistics that show that

00:12:16.253 --> 00:12:18.242
that's not the case?

00:12:18.242 --> 00:12:21.099
- I have statistics that would absolutely

00:12:21.099 --> 00:12:23.256
make your head spin.

00:12:23.256 --> 00:12:25.248
If you're interrupted by email and phone,

00:12:25.248 --> 00:12:27.805
there was an experiment done
at Kings College, for example,

00:12:27.805 --> 00:12:30.001
that showed that people who were stoned

00:12:30.001 --> 00:12:31.585
scored six points better on an IQ test

00:12:31.585 --> 00:12:34.266
than people who were
interrupted by email and phone.

00:12:34.266 --> 00:12:35.433
- [Woman] Wow.

00:12:36.301 --> 00:12:37.134
Good to know.

00:12:37.134 --> 00:12:39.229
- Good to know, 26 percent
of people in the American

00:12:39.229 --> 00:12:41.424
workforce are on the verge
of a nervous breakdown.

00:12:41.424 --> 00:12:42.731
The system is not working.

00:12:42.731 --> 00:12:45.251
It's not a question of,
if I should do this.

00:12:45.251 --> 00:12:46.598
It's a question of when.

00:12:46.598 --> 00:12:49.265
It is the only real alternative.

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:52.819
One simple step that people
can take, a baby step,

00:12:52.819 --> 00:12:57.781
to prove the concept, is to
simply use an autoresponder.

00:12:57.781 --> 00:13:00.837
Set up an autoresponder that
tells everyone who emails you,

00:13:00.837 --> 00:13:02.551
I will be checking email twice a day.

00:13:02.551 --> 00:13:03.610
- [Woman] That's a great idea.

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:05.518
- At 11:00 and 4:00 PM.

00:13:05.518 --> 00:13:07.714
If you require a more
urgent response before

00:13:07.714 --> 00:13:09.833
one of those two times,
call me on my cell phone.

00:13:09.833 --> 00:13:11.258
- [Woman] Okay, if I
start losing customers,

00:13:11.258 --> 00:13:12.193
I'm going to call you, Tim.

00:13:12.193 --> 00:13:13.533
- You can call me.
- Okay?

00:13:13.533 --> 00:13:15.687
- What's going to happen,
instead of losing customers,

00:13:15.687 --> 00:13:17.970
you'll get more done in the next 48 hours

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:20.046
than you would in the next two weeks.

00:13:20.046 --> 00:13:23.298
- In a world where people
expect immediate responses,

00:13:23.298 --> 00:13:27.465
oftentimes, and increasingly
so, you have to, I believe,

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:32.460
let small, bad things happen, constantly,

00:13:32.460 --> 00:13:34.738
to have any agenda of your own,

00:13:34.738 --> 00:13:37.051
and to get the big, positive things done.

00:13:37.051 --> 00:13:41.218
It's recognizing that to
prevent all hurt feelings,

00:13:42.217 --> 00:13:44.701
all mistakes, all problems,
all of this is impossible.

00:13:44.701 --> 00:13:45.646
And if you try to do that,

00:13:45.646 --> 00:13:49.943
you'll never have a proactive
schedule of your own,

00:13:49.943 --> 00:13:50.920
is extremely important.

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:52.832
So, effectively, just
saying I'm going to accept

00:13:52.832 --> 00:13:54.499
the collateral damage

00:13:54.499 --> 00:13:57.340
and believe that what I'm embarking upon

00:13:57.340 --> 00:14:01.076
is worth more than those
minor or reversible problems.

00:14:01.076 --> 00:14:02.993
And then forging ahead.

00:14:03.918 --> 00:14:06.732
That's it. You got to
take a few flesh wounds.

00:14:06.732 --> 00:14:08.523
The five-minute journal is,

00:14:08.523 --> 00:14:10.234
it's a journal that
was created by a reader

00:14:10.234 --> 00:14:11.373
of The Four-Hour Work Week, actually,

00:14:11.373 --> 00:14:13.487
for those who have read the book.

00:14:13.487 --> 00:14:17.790
It was their muse, so one
of their cash-flow focused

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:21.678
businesses in the concept
of lifestyle design, but

00:14:21.678 --> 00:14:24.267
you take two and a half
minutes, or so, in the morning

00:14:24.267 --> 00:14:25.452
and then again at night.

00:14:25.452 --> 00:14:27.935
So one is, effectively, a
focusing and planning exercise.

00:14:27.935 --> 00:14:29.363
There's also a gratitude component,

00:14:29.363 --> 00:14:31.193
which I think is very critical

00:14:31.193 --> 00:14:35.741
for those of us who are
driven, Type A, achievers.

00:14:35.741 --> 00:14:39.600
It's very easy to constantly
be focused on the future.

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:41.186
And just to hit pause for a second,

00:14:41.186 --> 00:14:43.261
I heard someone say that depression is

00:14:43.261 --> 00:14:44.237
an obsession with the past,

00:14:44.237 --> 00:14:46.510
and anxiety is an
obsession with the future.

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:49.277
Well, if you look at achievers,

00:14:49.277 --> 00:14:51.715
they tend to be very future-focused.

00:14:51.715 --> 00:14:53.056
And as soon as they hit
a goal, they're like,

00:14:53.056 --> 00:14:55.937
don't have time to
celebrate this small win.

00:14:55.937 --> 00:15:00.775
This isn't good enough, bigger,
bigger, better, et cetera.

00:15:00.775 --> 00:15:03.571
That is a pattern that can
be very self destructive,

00:15:03.571 --> 00:15:06.825
even if you rack up a lot
of wins at the same time.

00:15:06.825 --> 00:15:09.064
So the gratitude component
is extremely critical.

00:15:09.064 --> 00:15:11.824
That takes about two and
a half minutes each day,

00:15:11.824 --> 00:15:15.721
and it also helps to
identify your focal points

00:15:15.721 --> 00:15:19.623
or your priorities so
that when, inevitably,

00:15:19.623 --> 00:15:22.589
that 10 percent that's left
of the monkey mind pops up

00:15:22.589 --> 00:15:24.783
to dance in front of you and distract you

00:15:24.783 --> 00:15:27.751
from your objectives
you set out for the day,

00:15:27.751 --> 00:15:29.008
you can return to that.

00:15:29.008 --> 00:15:30.025
And then, at the end of the day,

00:15:30.025 --> 00:15:33.320
it's basically a performance review.

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:36.129
I find it incredibly helpful, and

00:15:36.129 --> 00:15:38.449
a lot of ROI for the time invested.

00:15:38.449 --> 00:15:40.967
Large, uninterrupted blocks of time.

00:15:40.967 --> 00:15:43.284
You have to schedule,
if you are a creator,

00:15:43.284 --> 00:15:46.171
blocks of time that are,
at least, two to four hours

00:15:46.171 --> 00:15:47.550
or more in length.

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:51.085
No Frankentstein monster
of 20-minute breaks

00:15:51.085 --> 00:15:53.850
and 10-minute breaks
combined into three hours

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:56.454
will have the value of
an uninterrupted block

00:15:56.454 --> 00:15:57.835
of three hours.

00:15:57.835 --> 00:16:00.360
If you are trying to make
high-level decisions,

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:04.791
focus on time-consuming,
high-priority projects,

00:16:04.791 --> 00:16:07.027
push something to a next milestone,

00:16:07.027 --> 00:16:08.530
as a maker/creator.

00:16:08.530 --> 00:16:11.048
We all have the same 24 hours in a day

00:16:11.048 --> 00:16:12.511
and if you're like, I don't have time,

00:16:12.511 --> 00:16:14.178
I can't sleep, I need
to cut back on sleep,

00:16:14.178 --> 00:16:15.843
I need to do this, I need to do that,

00:16:15.843 --> 00:16:18.972
if you don't have time,
you don't have priorities.

00:16:18.972 --> 00:16:19.865
That's it.

00:16:19.865 --> 00:16:22.792
- Are you one of those entrepreneurs who

00:16:22.792 --> 00:16:25.149
likes to launch very early with a product

00:16:25.149 --> 00:16:29.131
which is not finished and get
as much feedback as you can,

00:16:29.131 --> 00:16:31.369
or are you, the other school is more like,

00:16:31.369 --> 00:16:34.501
you do it in secret, and you
test, do the market research,

00:16:34.501 --> 00:16:35.921
you do everything in secret and you launch

00:16:35.921 --> 00:16:37.626
when you consider it's really good?

00:16:37.626 --> 00:16:39.627
- I think that you can do both, actually.

00:16:39.627 --> 00:16:42.427
I think that it's possible
to get that early feedback,

00:16:42.427 --> 00:16:46.094
which is critical,
without making it public,

00:16:47.265 --> 00:16:48.646
in the sense that,

00:16:48.646 --> 00:16:51.048
you can work even with a
small group of friends,

00:16:51.048 --> 00:16:52.917
which is what I did with the book,

00:16:52.917 --> 00:16:55.806
but also with some of the
startups that I'm working with,

00:16:55.806 --> 00:16:56.987
get feedback from people

00:16:56.987 --> 00:16:59.143
who represent different demographics,

00:16:59.143 --> 00:17:02.025
then incorporate that into the beta,

00:17:02.025 --> 00:17:03.933
when you make it open.

00:17:03.933 --> 00:17:07.386
But I generally believe in fast sprints,

00:17:07.386 --> 00:17:10.720
let's just say, two-week
development sprints.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:13.974
So that might combine
with agile development,

00:17:13.974 --> 00:17:16.816
but do two-week sprints, push
something out, get feedback.

00:17:16.816 --> 00:17:18.727
Take a brief rest.

00:17:18.727 --> 00:17:20.962
Do another sprint, get a few features out,

00:17:20.962 --> 00:17:22.545
wait, get feedback.

00:17:23.886 --> 00:17:25.913
Google also does this
on a fairly large scale.

00:17:25.913 --> 00:17:27.743
When they launched
Google News, for example,

00:17:27.743 --> 00:17:30.747
they were wondering
whether they should sort by

00:17:30.747 --> 00:17:35.291
location or date, and there
were a few other variables.

00:17:35.291 --> 00:17:36.957
They decided that they would push it out

00:17:36.957 --> 00:17:39.882
and simply see how many requests
and complaints they got,

00:17:39.882 --> 00:17:41.592
related to each of those features.

00:17:41.592 --> 00:17:44.109
And almost no one asked for location.

00:17:44.109 --> 00:17:45.163
Most of them asked for dates.

00:17:45.163 --> 00:17:46.466
So that's the feature
that they built into it

00:17:46.466 --> 00:17:47.647
in the next iteration.

00:17:47.647 --> 00:17:50.131
So I believe in microtesting.

00:17:50.131 --> 00:17:51.836
I'd say that's generally my approach.

00:17:51.836 --> 00:17:54.397
Keep things simple, and when
you're looking for solutions,

00:17:54.397 --> 00:17:58.462
to try to remove things first
rather than to add things.

00:17:58.462 --> 00:18:01.022
This is a really, really
critical principle.

00:18:01.022 --> 00:18:04.353
And less can be more in
this particular respect,

00:18:04.353 --> 00:18:06.754
especially for behavioral change.

00:18:06.754 --> 00:18:08.913
This is a sign that I have
over one of my doorways

00:18:08.913 --> 00:18:10.831
in my house, Simplify.

00:18:10.831 --> 00:18:13.507
And above that I have
a knife. Why a knife?

00:18:13.507 --> 00:18:15.094
Because when you make a decision,

00:18:15.094 --> 00:18:17.495
the word decision is related to incision.

00:18:17.495 --> 00:18:19.038
It means to cut off.

00:18:19.038 --> 00:18:21.727
It means to cut away other options

00:18:21.727 --> 00:18:23.313
and to commit and to focus.

00:18:23.313 --> 00:18:24.816
And that's what I recommend all of you do

00:18:24.816 --> 00:18:26.078
with whatever that skill is that you have

00:18:26.078 --> 00:18:27.624
in your head still, hopefully.

00:18:27.624 --> 00:18:30.712
Perfection is achieved not when
there's nothing more to add,

00:18:30.712 --> 00:18:32.619
but when there's nothing
more to take away.

00:18:32.619 --> 00:18:35.202
Let's just go to this mat here.

00:18:37.217 --> 00:18:39.005
- [Jason] Oh wait, we got
to do it the right way.

00:18:39.005 --> 00:18:40.595
- Ah, I was going to catch you.

00:18:40.595 --> 00:18:41.428
- My Dutch friends (mumbles).

00:18:41.428 --> 00:18:42.438
- So the, yeah,

00:18:42.438 --> 00:18:43.822
this is a, so yeah,

00:18:43.822 --> 00:18:46.824
then I lean back, then we
bring the partner down.

00:18:46.824 --> 00:18:48.745
So apparently, the Dutch
acrobats get very upset

00:18:48.745 --> 00:18:50.462
if you don't do that.

00:18:50.462 --> 00:18:51.892
I've gotten to the point
where I'm like, you know what?

00:18:51.892 --> 00:18:53.730
I've had enough of the Dutch.

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:57.467
I love their kickboxing,
Ernesto Hoost, love you, but.

00:18:57.467 --> 00:19:00.433
From all the jiu jitsu,
I'm just like, flop.

00:19:00.433 --> 00:19:02.259
I've made a lot of, I'm
not going to say enemies.

00:19:02.259 --> 00:19:05.279
That doesn't really happen in Acro.

00:19:05.279 --> 00:19:07.684
I am standing with my feet

00:19:07.684 --> 00:19:08.902
reasonably close to your hips.

00:19:08.902 --> 00:19:11.302
- I want to be able to
touch my flier's feet.

00:19:11.302 --> 00:19:14.060
My feet are turned out
just below the hip bones.

00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:15.526
- I am standing straight up.

00:19:15.526 --> 00:19:17.926
It's almost as if, you've seen the UFC,

00:19:17.926 --> 00:19:20.044
you're like open guard on your back.

00:19:20.044 --> 00:19:21.262
This would be where I try to punch you

00:19:21.262 --> 00:19:23.171
and then you kick me in the
face, but that's not Acro.

00:19:23.171 --> 00:19:24.391
That's MMA.

00:19:24.391 --> 00:19:26.946
All right, and then
flier, fingers forward.

00:19:26.946 --> 00:19:28.490
- You got it. You got the
acronyms and everything.

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:30.562
- Just so people, I learned,

00:19:30.562 --> 00:19:34.706
I'm a young Padawan. I'm
just learning what I can.

00:19:34.706 --> 00:19:36.536
- The most important thing
is that you understand

00:19:36.536 --> 00:19:38.244
when to be water and when to be earth,

00:19:38.244 --> 00:19:39.462
and what parts of your body.

00:19:39.462 --> 00:19:41.210
So I'm going to be watery with my arms,

00:19:41.210 --> 00:19:42.469
and really sturdy with my legs.

00:19:42.469 --> 00:19:43.302
So we'll take a deep breath.

00:19:43.302 --> 00:19:44.135
(inhales)

00:19:44.135 --> 00:19:45.763
On the exhale, I receive with everything

00:19:45.763 --> 00:19:48.727
and then I push up with the legs.

00:19:48.727 --> 00:19:50.398
Here, a lot of times, the
flier will want to put

00:19:50.398 --> 00:19:52.222
the hands down and practice
their handstand training.

00:19:52.222 --> 00:19:53.808
That means they're a control freak.

00:19:53.808 --> 00:19:55.676
- [Tim] Yeah.

00:19:55.676 --> 00:19:58.890
So you fold the top of their
hands down to the floor,

00:19:58.890 --> 00:20:01.697
so at this point, those people listening,

00:20:01.697 --> 00:20:04.820
I am supported on Jason's feet.

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:06.202
I'm completely upside down.

00:20:06.202 --> 00:20:09.452
I'm basically in a L-straddle position,

00:20:10.503 --> 00:20:14.848
for those of you who know
anything about gymnastics.

00:20:14.848 --> 00:20:17.281
So this is Folded Leaf.

00:20:17.281 --> 00:20:20.248
Sometimes, in hushed tones,

00:20:20.248 --> 00:20:22.602
jokingly referred to as Leaf Blower.

00:20:22.602 --> 00:20:23.740
- Oh my god, I didn't know that.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:25.804
I was going to come and blog that.

00:20:25.804 --> 00:20:26.637
- [Tim] I'm trying to help people

00:20:26.637 --> 00:20:28.033
with the images here.

00:20:28.033 --> 00:20:29.703
So this is Scarecrow, right?

00:20:29.703 --> 00:20:30.965
- This is Scarecrow.

00:20:30.965 --> 00:20:32.268
And basically what I do, as a base,

00:20:32.268 --> 00:20:35.683
is I try to let your body unwind patterns.

00:20:35.683 --> 00:20:37.717
So as I bounce and shake, a lot of times

00:20:37.717 --> 00:20:40.275
our mind is connected to muscle groups,

00:20:40.275 --> 00:20:42.674
and flexing them when
they don't need to be.

00:20:42.674 --> 00:20:44.749
So the true therapist is gravity,

00:20:44.749 --> 00:20:48.916
which helps to let your
spine hang like a plumb line.

00:20:50.786 --> 00:20:54.322
- [Tim] And could we show, for instance,

00:20:54.322 --> 00:20:56.314
let's see here, Super Yogi?

00:20:56.314 --> 00:20:57.764
- Sure.

00:20:57.764 --> 00:21:00.240
So Tim likes traction,

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:02.189
so what I can do is, I
can let my legs come off

00:21:02.189 --> 00:21:03.567
my 90 degree.

00:21:03.567 --> 00:21:04.829
His weight will start falling back.

00:21:04.829 --> 00:21:05.662
We'll take a deep breath together.

00:21:05.662 --> 00:21:06.501
(inhales)

00:21:06.501 --> 00:21:08.409
On the exhale, my legs go back

00:21:08.409 --> 00:21:11.215
and I resist with the arms.

00:21:11.215 --> 00:21:12.105
It's okay?
- Mm-hmm.

00:21:12.105 --> 00:21:14.548
- Two more, inhale.
(inhales)

00:21:14.548 --> 00:21:18.353
Exhale, so I'm pushing the legs
back and giving resistance.

00:21:18.353 --> 00:21:20.630
I got a little wrist action there.

00:21:20.630 --> 00:21:22.801
One more time, inhale.
(inhales)

00:21:22.801 --> 00:21:26.583
And exhale.
(exhales)

00:21:26.583 --> 00:21:27.922
And then where I love to go

00:21:27.922 --> 00:21:30.564
is to open up your triceps
and your shoulders.

00:21:30.564 --> 00:21:32.511
So I'm going to place my
hands under his elbows.

00:21:32.511 --> 00:21:36.678
He can bend the elbows and
the knees at the same rate.

00:21:37.637 --> 00:21:38.900
Okay, bring the elbows closer.

00:21:38.900 --> 00:21:40.122
Does that feel okay on your shoulder?

00:21:40.122 --> 00:21:42.150
- [Tim] Yeah, maybe be a
little careful on the left.

00:21:42.150 --> 00:21:42.983
- Yeah.

00:21:42.983 --> 00:21:43.930
- [Tim] Just had it
reconstructed, but yeah.

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:44.784
This feels fine.

00:21:44.784 --> 00:21:46.766
- [Jason] And in general, where
the body can be vulnerable

00:21:46.766 --> 00:21:48.839
in the flying is in the
shoulders and the lower back,

00:21:48.839 --> 00:21:50.831
so communication is really important,

00:21:50.831 --> 00:21:53.467
and just listening to your body.

00:21:53.467 --> 00:21:54.300
All good?

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:55.967
- All good.
- Sweet.

00:21:57.664 --> 00:21:59.819
Side bending is also a
thing that can really help

00:21:59.819 --> 00:22:02.879
the health of the lower back.

00:22:02.879 --> 00:22:04.625
And what's different with
the therapeutic flying

00:22:04.625 --> 00:22:07.438
from yoga is, your body
doesn't have to be engaged.

00:22:07.438 --> 00:22:10.205
You're not using your
muscles as the flier,

00:22:10.205 --> 00:22:13.779
so you don't have to
work uphill, basically.

00:22:13.779 --> 00:22:15.279
- [Tim] All right.

00:22:17.475 --> 00:22:18.613
- Ya mon.
- Ya mon.

00:22:18.613 --> 00:22:22.530
So those are some basics
of therapeutic flying.

00:22:23.849 --> 00:22:25.352
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:22:25.352 --> 00:22:27.711
I'd love to know, what did
you take from this video?

00:22:27.711 --> 00:22:30.759
What was the most important
lesson that you learned

00:22:30.759 --> 00:22:32.552
that blew your mind, that you're going to

00:22:32.552 --> 00:22:35.927
immediately apply to your
life or your business somehow?

00:22:35.927 --> 00:22:37.636
Please leave it down
in the comments below.

00:22:37.636 --> 00:22:38.896
I'm super curious to find out.

00:22:38.896 --> 00:22:40.240
Also, if you want to nominate someone

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:43.128
for the next Top Ten video,
please check the description

00:22:43.128 --> 00:22:45.732
for a link to a video where
you can vote for people

00:22:45.732 --> 00:22:47.524
and put in your suggestions, as well.

00:22:47.524 --> 00:22:49.724
I also want to give a quick
shout out to John Sonmez

00:22:49.724 --> 00:22:51.835
from the Simple Programmer
YouTube channel.

00:22:51.835 --> 00:22:54.355
John, thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:22:54.355 --> 00:22:56.917
Your One Word, and for
making that awesome video

00:22:56.917 --> 00:22:57.941
on your channel.

00:22:57.941 --> 00:22:59.570
I really, really appreciate the support

00:22:59.570 --> 00:23:01.359
and I'm glad that you enjoyed the read.

00:23:01.359 --> 00:23:05.387
- And recently, I just had
the opportunity to read

00:23:05.387 --> 00:23:07.138
a book that he put out,

00:23:07.138 --> 00:23:10.144
which I just did a video review on.

00:23:10.144 --> 00:23:12.096
- So thank you guys, again, for watching.

00:23:12.096 --> 00:23:13.273
I believe in you.

00:23:13.273 --> 00:23:15.306
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:23:15.306 --> 00:23:17.499
and whatever your one word is.

00:23:17.499 --> 00:23:19.916
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:23:26.168 --> 00:23:27.835
- For every problem,

00:23:28.746 --> 00:23:31.185
the problem, itself,
presents an opportunity

00:23:31.185 --> 00:23:33.749
that dwarfs the problem.

00:23:33.749 --> 00:23:35.905
That is to say that if
I had only looked at

00:23:35.905 --> 00:23:37.898
my failure with the audio book,

00:23:37.898 --> 00:23:40.138
and viewed it as a failure
in the product, let's say,

00:23:40.138 --> 00:23:43.264
and not in the process, I
wouldn't have taken away much.

00:23:43.264 --> 00:23:46.032
Instead, I realized that
my approach wasn't right,

00:23:46.032 --> 00:23:47.532
fixed my approach, and
that has led to a lot

00:23:47.532 --> 00:23:49.193
of great successes.

00:23:49.193 --> 00:23:52.161
If you get a thousand die-hard, true fans

00:23:52.161 --> 00:23:53.590
who love your product, pay for it,

00:23:53.590 --> 00:23:55.373
and are relatively price-insensitive,

00:23:55.373 --> 00:23:56.835
you can always scale up from there.

00:23:56.835 --> 00:23:59.435
If you try to aim for, as they would say,

00:23:59.435 --> 00:24:02.234
boiling the ocean, you're
going to run out of money,

00:24:02.234 --> 00:24:03.627
and your advertising, your marketing

00:24:03.627 --> 00:24:05.132
will be very imprecise.

00:24:05.132 --> 00:24:07.904
It will be designed
subconsciously or consciously

00:24:07.904 --> 00:24:11.232
not to offend anyone, and as a result,

00:24:11.232 --> 00:24:13.788
you will have no die-hard fans.

00:24:13.788 --> 00:24:15.455
A lot of really good

00:24:16.876 --> 00:24:20.940
entrepreneurs start as a
technician or tactician,

00:24:20.940 --> 00:24:22.729
are very, very good at one thing.

00:24:22.729 --> 00:24:25.694
Then they end up in a
managerial role that they hate.

00:24:25.694 --> 00:24:26.913
It doesn't mean you have to stay there.

00:24:26.913 --> 00:24:29.477
And you see a lot of folks,
like Evan Williams and others,

00:24:29.477 --> 00:24:31.751
who then, at some point, realize this

00:24:31.751 --> 00:24:35.251
and return to a more product-focused role,

00:24:36.299 --> 00:24:38.047
even if they are also the CEO

00:24:38.047 --> 00:24:41.377
making some high-level,
30,000-foot decisions.

00:24:41.377 --> 00:24:43.489
Okay, but, if you are a maker,

00:24:43.489 --> 00:24:44.599
if you've decided to be a maker,

00:24:44.599 --> 00:24:47.685
if you just happen to
be a maker or a creator,

00:24:47.685 --> 00:24:50.534
let's call it three- to
five-hour uninterrupted

00:24:50.534 --> 00:24:52.728
blocks of time are extremely critical.

00:24:52.728 --> 00:24:54.312
If you want to connect the dots,

00:24:54.312 --> 00:24:56.384
if you want to have the space

00:24:56.384 --> 00:24:58.985
to allow yourself to have original ideas,

00:24:58.985 --> 00:25:02.890
or at least, original
combinations of ideas,

00:25:02.890 --> 00:25:05.571
you really need to block
out that time and protect it

00:25:05.571 --> 00:25:07.321
at least once a week.

00:25:08.295 --> 00:25:10.248
In Tools of Titans, there
are many people who do this,

00:25:10.248 --> 00:25:14.392
Ramit Sethi, for instance,
has a very, very successful

00:25:14.392 --> 00:25:16.386
multi-million dollar
business that he built

00:25:16.386 --> 00:25:20.771
out of a blog he started
long ago in college,

00:25:20.771 --> 00:25:23.532
which was very, very niche in its focus.

00:25:23.532 --> 00:25:26.374
He blocks out, I believe
it's every Wednesday

00:25:26.374 --> 00:25:28.895
for three to five hours of time,

00:25:28.895 --> 00:25:31.534
he'll block it out for learning.

00:25:31.534 --> 00:25:34.498
Noah Kagan, another entrepreneur,
does the same thing.

00:25:34.498 --> 00:25:37.949
So on Wednesdays, for me, I
have from 9:00 AM to 1:00 PM,

00:25:37.949 --> 00:25:40.797
this is pre-lunch, I have Creation.

00:25:40.797 --> 00:25:44.964
That means writing, recording,
or some similar aspect

00:25:46.694 --> 00:25:51.246
of, in my mind, creating with
my skill set and my assets.

00:25:51.246 --> 00:25:53.363
And it is extremely
important that I do that

00:25:53.363 --> 00:25:56.294
before I am barraged by inputs.

00:25:56.294 --> 00:25:58.688
In other words, and this
is true of Josh, as well,

00:25:58.688 --> 00:26:00.884
first thing in the morning,
he's doing journaling.

00:26:00.884 --> 00:26:04.172
Reid Hoffman, billionaire,
co-founder or founder

00:26:04.172 --> 00:26:06.611
of LinkedIn, same story.

00:26:06.611 --> 00:26:09.616
He will plant a seed in
his mind the night before,

00:26:09.616 --> 00:26:11.205
a problem he wants to solve,

00:26:11.205 --> 00:26:13.930
a project he wants to think
about improving, perhaps,

00:26:13.930 --> 00:26:15.430
and then waking up

00:26:17.751 --> 00:26:20.276
tabula rasa, complete blank slate,

00:26:20.276 --> 00:26:23.489
immediately working on that
problem with journaling,

00:26:23.489 --> 00:26:26.661
before any text messages,
before any email.

00:26:26.661 --> 00:26:28.489
Which is why, for instance,
I don't have email

00:26:28.489 --> 00:26:29.322
set up on my phone.

00:26:29.322 --> 00:26:30.841
I do not have mail set up on my iPhone.

00:26:30.841 --> 00:26:32.304
I do not get notifications.

00:26:32.304 --> 00:26:35.559
I also put my phone on Airplane
mode, for a lot of reasons.

00:26:35.559 --> 00:26:36.494
If you've read The Four-Hour Body,

00:26:36.494 --> 00:26:37.509
it explains some of the physical ones.

00:26:37.509 --> 00:26:40.276
But onto Airplane mode when I go to bed,

00:26:40.276 --> 00:26:42.759
and it stays in Airplane
mode until I am done

00:26:42.759 --> 00:26:45.196
with my Creation period.

00:26:45.196 --> 00:26:46.253
And then it comes on,

00:26:46.253 --> 00:26:49.014
because as soon as you
go into bullet-dodging

00:26:49.014 --> 00:26:52.752
or, like Wonder Woman,
bullet blocking mode

00:26:52.752 --> 00:26:56.451
with everyone else's agenda for your time,

00:26:56.451 --> 00:26:59.821
which is very often the
inbox or text messages,

00:26:59.821 --> 00:27:01.199
you're DOA, you're done.

00:27:01.199 --> 00:27:05.345
Your creativity is all
for naught, in general.

00:27:05.345 --> 00:27:09.772
So for me, for many people
who are, say, programming,

00:27:09.772 --> 00:27:13.825
for musicians, for creative
types, slack in the system.

00:27:13.825 --> 00:27:15.539
You have to create slack.

00:27:15.539 --> 00:27:17.120
You have to create space.

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:21.510
You have to create large,
uninterrupted blocks of time,

00:27:21.510 --> 00:27:24.323
and the only way to do that
is to put it on your calendar.

00:27:24.323 --> 00:27:26.351
If it's not on your
calendar, it's not real.

00:27:26.351 --> 00:27:27.574
You need to put it in your calendar

00:27:27.574 --> 00:27:29.959
and defend it, just like
you would anything else.

